**AI: The Capability Gap That’s Growing**

*Prepared for Greg Foran, CEO, The Kroger Co.*

## **The Competitive Reality**

Walmart has reorganized its technology leadership around AI as a core enterprise capability. They’ve created dedicated AI leadership roles at EVP and SVP level, migrated to cloud-native data infrastructure, and deployed internal AI tools — merchandising analytics, coding assistants, advertising optimization, inventory management — that have achieved rapid organic adoption across 900,000 associates. Their four “super agents” serve customers, associates, suppliers, and developers. The customer-facing agent has been used by 50% of Walmart app users and drives 35% higher average order values. Internal AI productivity tools went viral across departments because they’re genuinely useful. The talent flywheel is accelerating — top AI builders want to work where the problems are hard and the leadership understands the opportunity.

Kroger’s AI posture today is consumer-facing features without enterprise infrastructure. The January 2026 Google Cloud expansion for a customer-facing shopping assistant is a good step, and the March 2026 elevation of Milen Mahadevan to Chief Data & AI Officer signals intent. But the CDAIO role still reports inside the existing technology organization, not directly to the CEO. There is no AI expertise on the board. AI at Kroger today sits in pockets — inventory pilots in ~35 stores, dynamic batching for pickup orders, retail media personalization through 84.51°. It’s not yet changing how merchants work, how associates operate, or how suppliers engage with the company.

## **The Supplier Platform Risk**

Walmart is building AI tools that suppliers will use across their entire business — not just the Walmart account. Demand forecasting, assortment planning, promotional optimization, advertising management. Once embedded in supplier workflows, these tools create enormous switching costs. Kroger risks becoming a secondary customer managed through a competitor’s AI infrastructure.

Kroger’s counter-positioning: leverage marketplace partnerships (Instacart, DoorDash, Uber Eats) and become the platform-friendly grocer these companies want to build with. This requires AI-literate leadership and strategic intent.

## **What Needs to Happen**

- **Appoint AI leadership that reports to the CEO.** AI can’t be a sub-function of the team that’s struggling to fix the website. The CDAIO role needs to be a peer to the CFO and COO — owning AI strategy across merchandising, supply chain, store operations, and supplier partnerships. Walmart made this move 9 months ago. Kroger needs to complete it.

- **Add AI expertise to the board.** Recruit a board member who has built and led AI at scale. The current board lacks the technical depth to evaluate AI investments or hold management accountable on this trajectory.

- **Reallocate capital toward AI.** The $2.5B Ocado impairment is a painful lesson in misallocated capital. With $3.8–$4.0B in planned capex this year, AI must be a visible, named investment priority — not buried inside IT budgets.

- **Elevate 84.51° from subsidiary to platform.** Kroger’s data science capability is world-class but siloed. Integrate it as an enterprise AI platform rather than operating it as a separate analytics service.

- **Deploy associate-facing AI tools.** Start with internal productivity — coding assistants, merchandising analytics, scheduling optimization. These build AI literacy across the organization and create the culture of adoption that Walmart already has. 400,000+ associates. Fastest ROI in the company.

- **Expand marketplace partnerships into AI.** Kroger’s relationships with Instacart, DoorDash, and Uber Eats are a competitive advantage. Extend them beyond delivery into shared AI infrastructure: catalogue intelligence, logistics optimization, demand forecasting.

- **Build a supplier-facing AI platform.** Kroger Precision Marketing and 84.51° have world-class data. Turn that into tools suppliers actually use to run their business, before Walmart’s platform becomes the default.

- **Engage external AI advisors.** Kroger has an opportunity to bring in advisors who understand AI in retail at the highest level. Prakhar Mehrotra — currently SVP and Global Head of AI at PayPal, formerly Corporate VP of Applied AI at Walmart Global Tech — is one example. He knows the Kroger ecosystem (PayPal is a payment partner), he knows Greg from Walmart, and he’s one of the most visible voices on agentic commerce in the industry. An advisory engagement or board observer role would be a high-signal, low-friction move.

**Bottom line: **Bill’s paper tells Greg what’s broken operationally. Your perspective tells him who to trust and how to navigate the politics. This section tells him what’s coming that neither of those cover — and it’s coming fast. If we put all three together, it’s the most complete transformation brief he’ll get from anyone outside the building.

The Ocado experience showed what happens when Kroger makes a multi-billion-dollar technology bet that misreads the market. The AI strategy should be the opposite: capital-light, partnership-driven, and focused on building real organizational capability rather than purchasing a vendor’s product.